RAPIDITY: THE FUNDAMENTAL QUANTITY
============================================================

  Rapidity: phi = arctanh(v) = (1/2)*ln((1+v)/(1-v)) = (1/2)*ln(Q(v))
  Inverse:  v = tanh(phi)
  Cayley:   Q(v) = e^{2*phi}

  ADDITION of rapidities = MULTIPLICATION of Q-values
  = COMPOSITION of velocities (relativistic addition)
  Rapidity is the LOG of our theory. It linearizes everything.

============================================================
1. RAPIDITY OF EVERY NATURAL NUMBER
============================================================

  The Cayley address of n is x_n = (n-1)/(n+1).
  Its rapidity is arctanh(x_n) = arctanh((n-1)/(n+1)).
  = (1/2)*ln(n^2/(2n/(n+1))... let's compute.
  Actually: (1+x)/(1-x) = (1+(n-1)/(n+1))/(1-(n-1)/(n+1))
  = (2n/(n+1))/(2/(n+1)) = n. So Q(x_n) = n.
  Therefore: rapidity of n = (1/2)*ln(n).

  THE RAPIDITY OF n IS HALF ITS LOGARITHM.

  n    address x_n    rapidity = ln(n)/2     e^(2*rapidity)
  ------------------------------------------------------------
    1     0.000000         0.000000                1.0000
    2     0.333333         0.346574                2.0000
    3     0.500000         0.549306                3.0000
    4     0.600000         0.693147                4.0000
    5     0.666667         0.804719                5.0000
    6     0.714286         0.895880                6.0000
    7     0.750000         0.972955                7.0000
    8     0.777778         1.039721                8.0000
    9     0.800000         1.098612                9.0000
   10     0.818182         1.151293               10.0000
   11     0.833333         1.198948               11.0000
   12     0.846154         1.242453               12.0000
   13     0.857143         1.282475               13.0000
   14     0.866667         1.319529               14.0000
   15     0.875000         1.354025               15.0000

  Rapidity is LOGARITHMIC in n. The natural numbers are
  EQUALLY SPACED on the log scale, which means their rapidities
  form an ARITHMETIC PROGRESSION with common difference ln(r)/2
  where r is the ratio between consecutive naturals.

  Between n and n+1: delta_rapidity = ln(1+1/n)/2 ~ 1/(2n).
  This is the HARMONIC SERIES! The rapidity gaps between
  consecutive integers decrease harmonically.

============================================================
2. RAPIDITY OF MUSICAL INTERVALS
============================================================

  Musical interval (n+1)/n has Cayley pre-image 1/(2n+1).
  Rapidity of the interval = arctanh(1/(2n+1))
  = (1/2)*ln(Q(1/(2n+1))) = (1/2)*ln((n+1)/n)

  Interval      Ratio   Rapidity    Cents
  --------------------------------------------------
  octave            2/ 1     0.346574    1200.0
  perfect fifth     3/ 2     0.202733     702.0
  perfect fourth    4/ 3     0.143841     498.0
  major third       5/ 4     0.111572     386.3
  minor third       6/ 5     0.091161     315.6
  major second      9/ 8     0.058892     203.9
  minor second      16/15     0.032269     111.7

  The rapidity of a musical interval = (1/2)*ln(frequency ratio).
  STACKING intervals = ADDING rapidities.
  Fifth + fourth = octave: ln(3/2)/2 + ln(4/3)/2 = ln(2)/2. CHECK!
  Verify: 0.202733 + 0.143841 = 0.346574 = ln(2)/2 = 0.346574

  Major third + minor third = fifth:
  0.111572 + 0.091161 = 0.202733 = ln(3/2)/2 = 0.202733

  CONSONANCE = LOW RAPIDITY.
  The most consonant intervals have the smallest rapidities.
  Rapidity IS the measure of musical tension.

